a='content/os/01-processes/proc-fork-exec-wait.md';b=a.replace('.md','.questions.json');src=['https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man2/fork.2.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man7/pid_namespaces.7.html','https://man7.org/linux/man-pages/man3/posix_spawn.3.html','https://redis.io/docs/latest/operate/oss_and_stack/management/optimization/latency/','https://docs.docker.com/reference/cli/docker/container/stop/']
for old,new in [('The only way to tell them apart is the return value:','The return value directly identifies which branch to take (PIDs and other attributes also differ):'),('This prints `child 15` then `parent 10`.','Assuming successful calls and no interruption of the wait, this prints `child 15` then `parent 10`.'),('nothing one writes is visible to the other.','private-memory changes are separate; explicitly shared mappings and external resources remain shared.'),('In general, n forks in a row give 2ⁿ processes.','Assuming every fork succeeds and every process executes the remaining calls, n successive forks give 2ⁿ processes.'),('file locks held with `fcntl`','process-associated record locks held with `fcntl` (open-file-description locks are inherited)'),('Every writable page is marked **read-only** in both.','Private writable mappings are protected for COW; mappings intentionally shared with `MAP_SHARED` retain shared semantics.'),('The kernel copies just that one 4 KiB page','For a shared private page with a 4 KiB base-page mapping, the kernel normally copies that page'),("For a small shell that's well under a millisecond. For a process with tens of gigabytes of memory it can take tens to hundreds of milliseconds (Redis's latency docs report around 10 ms per GB on typical hardware), during which the parent is frozen.","Actual cost depends on populated page tables, page sizes, allocator state, hardware and kernel. The calling thread cannot continue past fork until it returns; other threads are not universally frozen for that entire period."),('Redis recommends disabling transparent huge pages, because then each write copies a 2 MiB page instead of 4 KiB.','Redis latency documentation warns that transparent huge pages can worsen COW latency and memory usage. Copying versus splitting and page sizes depend on the kernel; not every small write necessarily copies 2 MiB.'),('    dup2(fd, 1);     /* stdout -> file */\n    close(fd);','    if (fd < 0) _exit(126);\n    if (dup2(fd, 1) < 0) _exit(126);\n    if (fd != 1) close(fd);'),('if (WIFEXITED(status))','if (done < 0) { /* check errno; retry EINTR */\n    perror("waitpid");\n} else if (WIFEXITED(status))'),('Shells add their own conventions on top:','Bash and several other shells use these conventions (other shells may encode signal deaths differently):'),('So `$?` of 137 is 128 + 9: killed by `SIGKILL`, very often by the OOM killer or by `docker stop` after its timeout. 143 is 128 + 15, `SIGTERM`.','In Bash, signal 9 can produce 137 and signal 15 can produce 143, but explicit `exit(137)` or `exit(143)` produce the same values. Status alone cannot prove a signal or OOM cause; inspect wait status or runtime diagnostics.'),('A zombie uses no memory or CPU, only a slot in the process table and a PID.','A zombie has no executing user code or normal address space, but retains kernel bookkeeping memory and a PID.'),('Enough of them exhaust PIDs (`/proc/sys/kernel/pid_max`, often 4,194,304 on 64-bit systems but sometimes 32,768)','Enough of them can exhaust available PIDs or other process limits'),('If PID 1 exits, the kernel panics.','If initial-namespace init exits, Linux panics. If a nested PID namespace\'s init exits, the kernel kills the remaining processes in that namespace instead.'),('The kernel doesn\'t deliver signals to PID 1 that it hasn\'t installed a handler for; this protects it from an accidental `kill`.','Init has special signal handling: ordinary terminating signals with default dispositions are generally ignored. Namespace and sender matter; ancestor PID namespaces can forcibly deliver SIGKILL/SIGSTOP to a nested namespace init.'),('so `docker stop` waits 10 seconds and then sends `SIGKILL`.','so Docker waits for its configured timeout (10 seconds by default for Linux containers) and then sends SIGKILL. The configured stop signal can differ from SIGTERM.'),('- `wait` collects an 8-bit exit status; shells report signal deaths as 128 + N, so 137 means `SIGKILL`.','- `wait` distinguishes normal exit from signal death. Bash commonly displays 128 + signal number, but a displayed 137 alone is ambiguous.'),('## Key takeaways','> [!note] Content gap: fork performance\n> No reproducible fork benchmark is supplied for this machine. Universal milliseconds-per-GB figures are omitted. Short snippets assume successful process creation and suitable headers unless they explicitly handle errors; production code must check failures and interrupted waits.\n\n## Key takeaways')]:edit(a,old,new,'Correct fork inheritance, COW scope, wait/redirection error handling and exit/init semantics.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q3','prompt','Bash reports status 137 for a command, and wait-status or runtime metadata confirms it died from a signal rather than calling exit normally. Which signal does the usual Bash encoding indicate?','137 alone is ambiguous; provide evidence needed for unique answer.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q3','explanation','Explicit exit(137) can yield the same Bash status, but the question separately confirms a signal death. Without that confirmation, status 137 alone would be ambiguous.','Remove frequency-based dismissal of a valid alternative.',src,option=3)
d=json.loads(Path(b).read_text());q=next(q for q in d if q['id'].endswith('-q3'))
qe(b,q['id'],'workedExample',q['workedExample'].replace('shells encode that as','Bash encodes that as').replace('In Kubernetes, `OOMKilled` with exit code 137 is the same thing.','Status 137 alone does not identify an OOM event; inspect the runtime reason and other evidence.'),'Distinguish shell convention and actual termination evidence.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q4','workedExample',next(q for q in json.loads(Path(b).read_text()) if q['id'].endswith('-q4'))['workedExample'].replace('The order of the two `a b` blocks could interleave in principle, but each process writes its whole buffer in one `write`.','Under the stated conventional buffering and one-write flush, each four-byte block is below PIPE_BUF and the two pipe writes do not interleave. Output assumptions include successful fork and normal exit.'),'Small pipe writes are atomic; remove contradictory interleaving assertion.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q9','prompt','A Python app runs as PID 1 in a Linux Docker container without a SIGTERM handler. Assume Docker uses SIGTERM and a 10-second stop timeout. Stop reaches the timeout and the app never runs graceful shutdown code. Which explanation and remedy fit?','Make diagnosis and timeout explicit rather than unique from symptoms.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q9','workedExample','Under the supplied stop policy, Docker sends SIGTERM and later SIGKILL. Namespace init ignores ordinary default-action SIGTERM; an application handler is needed for graceful cleanup. A tiny init can forward signals and reap orphaned descendants, but moving the app behind an init does not by itself create application cleanup logic: install the appropriate handler too. Ancestor namespaces can send SIGKILL to nested init.','Forwarding termination is not equivalent to executing graceful shutdown logic.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q10','explanation','COW can duplicate much of the private dataset while the snapshot child retains the earlier pages. About 40 GB is a simplified dataset estimate, not a complete upper bound on process/host memory; page tables, buffers and other allocations add overhead. THP behaviour depends on the kernel.','Remove universal 2MiB copy and hard host-memory cap.',src,option=2)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q10','workedExample','With a hypothetical 20 GB private dataset, copying nearly every shared page can add roughly another 20 GB while the snapshot child exists. Provision additional headroom for page tables, allocator and I/O overhead. Follow Redis guidance on THP and test the workload. vm.overcommit_memory=1 can change fork admission policy but does not create RAM or prevent later OOM.','Overcommit setting does not reduce actual memory requirement.',src)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q11','text','Plain fork followed by exec can duplicate substantial populated page-table state before the child replaces its address space','Remove unverified timing range and all-mappings assertion.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q11','explanation','COW avoids eagerly copying private page contents, but fork still has page-table and task-creation work. Its cost depends on mappings and page sizes; a measurement is needed.','Avoid unsupported absolute cost model.',src,option=0)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q11','explanation','There is no universal dominant step. Avoiding fork page-table work can help a large parent, but exec loading and other overhead must be measured.','Remove unmeasured claim that fork always dominates exec.',src,option=4)
qe(b,'proc-fork-exec-wait-q11','workedExample','For an illustrative densely populated 30 GiB mapping using 4 KiB pages, there are 7,864,320 leaf PTEs; at 8 bytes each, leaf entries alone occupy 60 MiB. Upper levels, huge pages and sparse mappings change the total and copying cost. glibc posix_spawn since 2.24 uses a shared-address-space CLONE_VM|CLONE_VFORK path, avoiding that ordinary fork duplication. After a multithreaded fork, only the calling thread exists in the child; use async-signal-safe operations until exec. No universal milliseconds-per-GB benchmark is supplied.','Correct GB/GiB ambiguity and omit unverified runtime default/timing.',src)
